          deepDive: "**〜ばかりに** = *just because ~, all because ~*, and what follows is **always a bad or regrettable result**. ばかり keeps its sense of *only*: the speaker singles out one cause — often a small one — and laments that this one thing led to the outcome. The book's gloss says it: 〜だけが原因で（悪い結果になってしまって残念だ）. The feeling is *if only that hadn't been the case…*: 背が2センチ足りないばかりに、警察官になれなかった (*just because I was two centimeters too short, I couldn't become a police officer*).\n\nConnection: plain form + ばかりに. な-adjectives and nouns take な (正直なばかりに, *just because he's honest*) or, more formally, **である** (両親が有名人であるばかりに, example ④). Past forms are very common because the cause is usually something that happened: 本当のことを言ったばかりに (*just because I told the truth*).\n\nConstraints on the second half:\n- It must be an unwanted result: 〜てしまった, 〜なかった, 〜られた, 〜にくい. ✗よく勉強したばかりに合格できた → ✓よく勉強したおかげで合格できた (*thanks to studying hard, I passed*).\n- It states a fact or a state, not the speaker's will: no requests, invitations or intentions. ✗雨が降ったばかりに、家にいよう → ✓雨が降ったから、家にいよう (*it's raining, so let's stay home*). In the practice, 仲よくしてほしいものだ (a wish) cannot follow 仲が悪いばかりに; 仕事がしにくい (*it's hard to get work done*, a resulting state) can.\n\nA special shape: **V-たいばかりに** = *just because one wanted ~ so badly*. The desire drives someone to an extreme or costly action: 彼女に会いたいばかりに、毎日その店に通った (*he went to that shop every day just because he wanted to see her*). Here the second half is the effort or sacrifice, not necessarily a misfortune.\n\nDon't confuse it with:\n- **〜せいで / 〜ために**: also the cause of a bad result, but neutral about scale; せいで points the finger: 雨のせいで試合が中止になった (*the game was called off because of the rain*). ばかりに adds *only because of that one thing* and regret.\n- **〜おかげで**: the cause of a good result — the positive counterpart.\n- **V-たばかり** (N3), *have just done*: 入社したばかりだ (*I've just joined the company*) — a time expression, no に.\n- **〜ばかりだ** (Plus under #115), *keeps getting (worse)*: 悪くなるばかりだ (*it just keeps getting worse*).\n\nCommon mistake: ✗親切なばかりに、みんなに好かれている (*he's liked by everyone just because he's kind*) — a good result, so use から or おかげで. But 親切なばかりに、いつも仕事を押しつけられる (*just because he's kind, people always dump work on him*) is fine.\n\nJLPT tip: in 問題1, when the first clause is a single cause and the second ends in a disappointment, ばかりに beats からには (#18), ものの (#39) and にもかかわらず (#21): 卒業論文が間に合わなかったばかりに、卒業が半年遅れてしまった (*just because my thesis wasn't ready in time, my graduation was delayed half a year*). からには needs a resolution or duty after it, and ものの and にもかかわらず introduce a result that goes *against* the first clause.",
